nothing: 0 (0 bytes, 0 clusters)
ASCII, one column each: 5 (5 bytes, 5 clusters)
a precomposed accent is still one character: 5 (6 bytes, 5 clusters)
and so is a combining one: the mark is zero: 5 (7 bytes, 5 clusters)
a combining mark on its own: 0 (2 bytes, 1 clusters)
CJK ideographs are wide: 4 (6 bytes, 2 clusters)
kana are wide: 10 (15 bytes, 5 clusters)
Hangul syllables are wide: 4 (6 bytes, 2 clusters)
and so is one spelled out in jamo, once: 2 (9 bytes, 1 clusters)
fullwidth Latin: 6 (9 bytes, 3 clusters)
halfwidth katakana punctuation is narrow: 3 (9 bytes, 3 clusters)
an emoji: 2 (4 bytes, 1 clusters)
with a skin tone: one cluster, still two: 2 (8 bytes, 1 clusters)
a flag is a pair of neutral letters, drawn wide: 2 (8 bytes, 1 clusters)
two flags: 4 (16 bytes, 2 clusters)
a family joined with ZWJ: 2 (18 bytes, 1 clusters)
a keycap: the emoji presentation selector makes it wide: 2 (7 bytes, 1 clusters)
a trigram, Wide since Unicode 16: 2 (3 bytes, 1 clusters)
a control character has no width of its own: 0 (1 bytes, 1 clusters)
a zero width space: 2 (5 bytes, 3 clusters)
a soft hyphen prints: 1 (2 bytes, 1 clusters)
a sentence: 10 (12 bytes, 10 clusters)
halfwidth katakana: 1 (3 bytes, 1 clusters)
Thai with a vowel sign above: 1 (6 bytes, 1 clusters)
Cyrillic is narrow: 7 (14 bytes, 7 clusters)
an ambiguous-width arrow counts as narrow: 6 (11 bytes, 5 clusters)
